[E-REFL] Error: `byComputing` can't prove this
    ╭─[ reject/match-refines-goal-once.sw:23:37 ]
    │
 16 │ ╭─▶     match step n with
    ┆ ┆   
 18 │ ├─▶       | None => None
    │ │                          
    │ ╰────────────────────────── this `match`
    │ 
 23 │         | None => Witness (Cons k rest) byComputing
    │                                         ─────┬─────  
    │                                              ╰─────── `(match step n with …)` and `None` aren't equal
    │     
    │     Note 1: the left side is stuck on `n`: it can't compute further until `n` is known; a `match` on `n` (or `cases n`) splits it
    │     
    │     Note 2: the `match` on `step n` replaces it only where the goal shows it, as written or computed, not inside a call that is stuck: a record pattern that destructures the value, or a helper lemma taking the matched part as a variable, reaches the inner `match`
    │     
    │     Note 3: `byComputing` proves `a = b` only when both sides compute to the same value
    │     
    │     Note 4: the `match` inside `run` has generated equations, one for each way through it: `run.eq2.some`, `run.eq2.none`, each under the hypothesis its branch is taken under; `stitchwort doc run` lists them
    │     
    │     Note 5: `(match …)` is a `match` written inside `run`, marked "this `match`" above; the compiler turned it into a function that source can't name. The same `match` written again is another such function, equal to it only if neither calls the definition it's written in. To state a lemma about it, make it a local `def` in its arm and refer to it as `owner.name`, where `owner` is the definition it is written in (docs/syntax.md, "Proving things about a definition with an inner `match`")
────╯
